    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Thus, the Haseman-Elston test is quite simple: for n pairs of the same type of relatives, one regresses the squared difference of each pair on the fraction of alleles ibd at the marker locus. A significant negative slope for the resulting regression indicates linkage to a QTL. This is a one-sided test, as the null hypothesis (no linkage) is $ \\beta = 0 $ versus the alternative $ \\beta < 0 $.",
      "source_page": 529,
      "printed_page": 509,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter16:p025:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        85.0,
        135.0,
        1047.0,
        294.0
